    {
      "id": "zo6g7tv",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Volcanoes"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Volcanoes are classified by activity (Active, Dormant, Extinct) and by physical shape (Shield, Composite/Stratovolcano, Cinder Cone).",
        "Earthquake energy propagates via seismic body waves (P-waves and S-waves) and surface waves (L-waves).",
        "Primary (P) waves are longitudinal, fastest, and travel through solids, liquids, and gases; Secondary (S) waves are transverse and pass only through solids.",
        "The Circum-Pacific Belt (Ring of Fire) contains the highest concentration of active volcanoes and seismic activity worldwide.",
        "Earthquake magnitude is measured quantitatively on the logarithmic Richter Scale, whereas earthquake intensity is measured qualitatively on the Mercalli Scale."
      ],
      "id": "fpc2vjr"
    },
